WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.768
Last page, we defined a correct interleaving.

00:00:04.768 --> 00:00:07.536
Conflict-serializability.

00:00:07.536 --> 00:00:10.303
Now, where does the database enforce it?

00:00:10.303 --> 00:00:13.071
You type BEGIN TRANSACTION.

00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:15.839
That one line runs down a stack four layers deep.

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:18.800
Follow one COMMIT down the whole stack.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.761
At the top, your code.

00:00:21.761 --> 00:00:24.722
It runs the transaction and sees one clean operation.

00:00:24.722 --> 00:00:27.683
Below it, the database.

00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:30.644
Its lock manager takes an exclusive lock on the Likes row.

00:00:30.644 --> 00:00:33.605
It decides what to lock.

00:00:33.605 --> 00:00:36.566
Below that, the operating system.

00:00:36.566 --> 00:00:39.527
It grabs a mutex on the thread holding that lock.

00:00:39.527 --> 00:00:42.488
It decides how.

00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:45.449
At the bottom, the hardware.

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:48.410
Every lock above reduces to one instruction here.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.371
What is it?

00:00:51.371 --> 00:00:54.332
Compare-and-swap.

00:00:54.332 --> 00:00:57.293
Read a memory location, check it has not changed, write a new value, all in one step that cannot be split.

00:00:57.293 --> 00:01:00.254
Then the guarantee climbs back up.

00:01:00.254 --> 00:01:03.215
Atomic at the bottom, isolated at the top.

00:01:03.215 --> 00:01:07.819
So why not just use the operating system's locks and stop there?

00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:12.423
Because they solve a smaller problem.

00:01:12.423 --> 00:01:17.027
An OS lock is one bit, locked or unlocked, guarding a memory address it knows nothing about.

00:01:17.027 --> 00:01:21.632
The database lock manager works in the language of your data.

00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:26.236
It locks a whole table, a page, or a single row.

00:01:26.236 --> 00:01:30.840
A transaction can announce intent, I plan to write these rows, queue around me.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.444
And it resolves deadlocks on its own, across millions of transactions.

00:01:35.444 --> 00:01:38.910
Now the component that hands out the locks.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:42.375
The lock manager.

00:01:42.375 --> 00:01:45.841
Every transaction comes here to lock the exact rows it touches, and it owes two goals.

00:01:45.841 --> 00:01:49.306
Correct, equal to some serial order.

00:01:49.306 --> 00:01:52.772
And fast, overlapping the idle waits.

00:01:52.772 --> 00:01:56.237
Watch three transactions ask at once.

00:01:56.237 --> 00:01:59.703
T1 and T2 both want a lock on Row A.

00:01:59.703 --> 00:02:03.168
T3 wants Row B.

00:02:03.168 --> 00:02:06.634
The manager records every request in its lock table, a live map of which row each lock sits on, who holds it, and who is queued behind.

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:10.100
Read it straight off the picture.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.565
Row A is locked by T1, so T2 waits.

00:02:13.565 --> 00:02:17.031
Row B is free, so T3's lock is granted.

00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:20.496
That is the manager's first job.

00:02:20.496 --> 00:02:23.962
Grant the lock when the row is free, queue you behind the holder when it is taken.

00:02:23.962 --> 00:02:27.427
Two jobs wait ahead.

00:02:27.427 --> 00:02:30.893
Schedule many transactions fast, and break the deadlocks that locks make possible.

00:02:30.893 --> 00:02:37.975
So one clean transaction on top, four cooperating layers beneath, and a lock manager that grants the free rows and queues the rest.

00:02:37.975 --> 00:02:45.057
Next, we follow a single lock from request to release, on DB Locks Lifecycle.
